That's correct, although slightly oversimplifying matters. There are many  
ways of naming any monster or object in the game, depending on the player  
character's acquired knowledge etc. etc., so you don't know the word that  
goes in the blanks at the time the overall output function is called. And  
if you want to fill in the blanks first, then grammatify the sentence,  
your "form evaluation" has to deal with enormous complexity in its input,  
or else you have to rewrite to reduce the game's output to a controlled  
subset of a natural language.

A friend and I have been working on NetzHack, the German version of  
Nethack,
for about two years now. We both have day jobs and other hobbies ...
But we play it and we have big long bug and to-do lists. www.netzhack.org  
(note the 'z') will someday have all the details and downloads.

As to our strategy:

NetHack is an excellent example of how *not* to program for l10n.

Just think about the little function the() in objnam.c for example, and
look how it's used, then realize that each call must determine,
based on the word that follows "the" in the output sentence,
whther to prepend not "the", but one of {der die das den dem des},
depending on whether the noun is masculine, feminine or neuter,
and whether it is being used in the nominative, genitive, dative or
accusative case, and in the singular or plural.

Then realize that every article and adjective and noun in every output
sentence that names a monster or an object has to be similarly treated
at runtime.

Since I do not want to rewrite Nethack from the ground up, I used this
strategy:

1. Just translate output strings in the source code.
2. Add a german.c module with the inflection tables
    and functions for German grammar.
3. Access the German grammar through macros in a new
    header, german.h. For example, where we had

	pline("%s grabs your %s!", Mon_nam(mon), xname(obj));

   we now have somehting like

	pline("%s nimmt %s!", Mon_nam_nomsing(mon), your_xname_acc(obj));

   meaning "<nominative singular>(the monster) grabs <accusative>(your  
object)!"

One problem is that German has a few dozen different declension patterns  
for
nouns, and so we need a dictionary that links each noun to its declension  
pattern.
The dictionary is currently has 1055 nouns, and there are many repetitive  
searches going on. I'm not an experienced programmer, and I'm trying to  
keep the code simple, so rather than saving information about the noun or  
its inflection pattern in the calling game functions, I'm just planning to  
cache a few recent results of the lookups.

One blessing is that there are no plural monsters, so we've been able to  
leave proper conjugation of verbs on the to-do list for a long time.

